{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["<b>And Moses went up to Elohim.</b> From the day they came to Mount Sinai, the cloud covered the mountain and there was the glory of YHWH. This is what is written (Exodus 24:16), \"And the glory of YHWH dwelt upon Mount Sinai, and the cloud covered it six days\" — before the giving of the Torah (Yoma 4a). Therefore it says <b>and Moses went up to Elohim</b>, for he went up to the edge of the mountain to present himself before Him, but did not enter the thick cloud where Elohim was. And HaShem called to him from the top of the mountain: <b>Thus shall you say to the house of Jacob</b>. And Rabbi Abraham said (Ibn Ezra on Exodus 19:3) that \"and He called to him\" is inverted in order, meaning He called to him and he went up to Him. But this does not seem correct to me, for the call is <b>Thus shall you say to the house of Jacob</b>, and he would interpret \"and He called to him\" as meaning to say to him \"Thus shall you say to the house of Jacob.\" And this is not correct. Rather, the sense of <b>Go up to Elohim</b> and <b>and YHWH called to him</b> is that he went up to the glory of HaShem that dwelt upon the mountain, to speak to Israel the Ten Commandments, and in His great name He would speak with Moses, as in the sense of \"If there be a prophet among you\" etc. (Numbers 12:6–8)."], :book :exodus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 19, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:10:40.065294298Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}